-- The graded session for a herdr DST profile: `NOTE-2026-09-03-herdr-under-dst.md`
-- §7 step 2's DEMONSTRATION half. A `Delegate` runs through the REAL traced
-- driver with `motoko-ext-herdr` installed, and every herdr CLI call it makes is
-- served from `WorldState.ext_effects` instead of reaching the live multiplexer.
--
-- WHAT IT SETTLED, and it was the item that made step 2 risky. That note's §8
-- listed one inference it could not check: "no run has yet exercised a
-- `ToolProvider` dispatch end to end — register entry 19 — so the first attempt
-- may find a seam the compose run did not." Every other profile in the tree
-- mediates a `ResponseInterceptor` (compose) or nothing at all. There is no
-- seam: the dispatch arrives, the hook runs, the scripted answers are consumed
-- in order, and the delegation completes. The world clock proves the serving —
-- the tool result says the spawn took 0.6s, which is this file's own
-- 97+101+103+107+109+113ms of scripted durations and not a real herdr.
--
-- WHAT IT IS NOT, YET. There is no `ProfileDefinition`, no discovery, no
-- validate/replay pair and no CLASSIFICATION/STATEMENT/CLAIM lines. Those are
-- the profile RECORD's half of step 2 and each of their fields has to be
-- measured for herdr rather than copied from compose. This half is what the
-- record would be a claim about.
--
-- THE SEQUENCE IS THE FRAGILE PART AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. `ext_effects` is
-- served IN ORDER and never matched against argv, so this fixture pins the exact
-- call sequence a successful claude `Delegate` makes. A change to that sequence
-- breaks this file loudly, which is the point: writing it caught two real
-- things — the startup sweep's `pane list` coming FIRST (entry 0), and the
-- retry's baseline `agent get` landing between the readiness gate and the prompt
-- (entry 4), added hours earlier the same day.
